WEBVTT

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.610
A1C is a blood test used to understand glucose levels over time.

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:11.280
When we eat carbohydrates, the body breaks them down into glucose.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:14.590
Glucose then travels through the bloodstream.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.187
Insulin helps glucose move from the bloodstream into the body's cells,

00:00:18.227 --> 00:00:20.240
where it can be used for energy.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:22.620
While glucose circulates in the blood,

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:26.620
some attaches to hemoglobin inside red blood cells.

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:31.900
An A1C test measures the percentage of hemoglobin with glucose attached.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:35.590
Because red blood cells live for about 120 days,

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:38.801
A1C provides information about glucose exposure

00:00:38.841 --> 00:00:42.050
over roughly the past two to three months.

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:44.867
Care Gap Explorer uses recorded A1C results

00:00:44.907 --> 00:00:49.550
to help identify monitoring gaps in a synthetic diabetes cohort.
